1214: ## 21. Finite-cap chain attachment with both deck directions
1215: 
1216: This subsection concerns the actual E7 cap away from the collision
1217: neighbourhood. Write its order as \(m\), its signed norm as \(n\),
1218: and take \(N=km\) with \(k>0\). For the sphere markings here
1219: \(n=\pm1\). The same Bezout argument below retains every
1220: coprime signed norm. Write
1221: \[
1222:  A=v^{kn}r_0^nA_0,\qquad B=v^{km}r_0^mB_0,\qquad
1223:  A_0^m=B_0^n,\quad[A_0,B_0]=1.                      \tag{SZ.47}
1224: \]
1225: Choose \(u,w\in\mathbb Z\) with \(nu+mw=1\). The actual
1226: positive chain generator is
1227: \[
1228:  H=A^uB^w=v^kr_0H_0,\qquad H^m=B,\quad H^n=A.        \tag{SZ.48}
1229: \]
1230: In particular \(A\) shifts a chain index by \(kn\), including
1231: the negative shift for \(n=-1\). It is not replaced by \(H\)
1232: when retaining the finite root character.
1233: 
1234: The source/line/root pairings are exactly the existing catalogue
1235: branches: the good III* root \(m=4p\) belongs to
1236: \((a,b)=(1,2)\) in F41, and the reduced III root \(m=4p-1\)
1237: belongs to \((a,b)=(2,1)\) in K24. No cross-pairing of these
1238: two equations with the other source line is made. In the good
1239: root, \(G'_0=(0,0)\) is the marked nonzero two-torsion point
1240: on \(Y'^2=X'^3+X'\), so \(Q'_0=2G'_0=O\), and the recorded
1241: division branch is the one through the identity. In the
1242: reduced root, \(Q'=G'\) has the recorded nonidentity component
1243: class of order two; the odd order \(m\) preserves this class,
1244: and the source norm and division branch are precisely those
1245: constructed on the resolved III source in K24/F37.
1246: 
1247: The root source and line extend through the source value \(v=0\).
1248: For the good III* root \(m=4p\), the literal equation is
1249: \[
1250:  Y'^2=X'^3+X'+(1-2v)z^{2p}X'^2+v^2z^{6p},\qquad
1251:  G'=(0,vz^{3p}).
1252: \]
1253: For the reduced III root \(m=4p-1\), it is
1254: \[
1255:  Y'^2=X'^3+zX'+(1-2v)z^{2p}X'^2+v^2z^{6p}.
1256: \]
1257: The smooth good-root family, and the simultaneous minimal
1258: resolution of the reduced III root, retain the same marked
1259: Cartier and invariant-ideal maps as F41 and K24. Their coefficients
1260: are holomorphic in \(v\); the added terms have the displayed
1261: higher \(z\)-orders. The division section and finite averaging
1262: therefore extend on the same inverse multiplication branch.
1263: The norm ratio is a holomorphic unit: its generic central
1264: value is \(-2\) in the good-root case and \(+2\) in the reduced
1265: case, by (SZ.11)--(SZ.12). Since it is a function on the base,
1266: these values determine its nonvanishing on the full compact
1267: central source fibre. Its chosen analytic root and the finite
1268: character thus extend over a smaller \((z,v)\) polydisc.
1269: 
1270: On the actual root line use the full chain
1271: \[
1272:  X_j=V/v^j,\qquad Y_j=v^{j+1}/V,\qquad X_jY_j=v.
1273:                                                                \tag{SZ.49}
1274: \]
1275: The transition is
1276: \(X_{j+1}=Y_j^{-1},\ Y_{j+1}=vY_j\). Source and inverse
1277: line frames make these equations invariant under every
1278: transition of the actual line. The map \(H\) sends chart
1279: \(j\) to chart \(j+k\), applying \(r_0H_0\) to \(X_j\)
1280: and its inverse line map to \(Y_j\). Its source map is the
1281: actual root translation and deck map contained in \(H_0\).
1282: Thus \(A=H^n\) has its exact root character and signed shift.
1283: The action is free and properly discontinuous also at \(v=0\):
1284: a nonzero iterate changes the chain index, and a finite union
1285: of consecutive node and middle charts has a compact
1286: fundamental region over a closed smaller source tube.
1287: For \(v\ne0\) this quotient is literally the original cap.
1288: 
1289: The ordinary-to-cap overlap requires its logarithmic origins;
1290: one does not multiply a fractional power of \(v\) into a single
1291: central chart. Put
1292: \[
1293:  t=\frac{\log v}{2\pi i},\quad
1294:  s_1=\frac{\log q_1}{2\pi i},\quad
1295:  s_\infty=\frac{\log q_\infty}{2\pi i},\quad
1296:  h=1-T_0(v)q_\infty.
1297: \]
1298: Then \(q_1=q_\infty^{-1}h\), so
1299: \[
1300:  s_1=-s_\infty+\frac{\log h}{2\pi i}.
1301: \]
1302: The actual ordinary-to-cap translation at the rescaled parameter
1303: is
1304: \[
1305:  F=F_0+nk\,t\,s_\infty\delta .
1306: \]
1307: Here \(F_0\) is the actual source, Cartier and cap comparison
1308: at the fixed reference scalar. It is holomorphic in \(v\)
1309: through zero on this annulus. Use the two origins
1310: \[
1311:  o_{\rm ord}=nk\,t\,s_1\delta,\qquad
1312:  o_{\rm cap}=nk\,t\,\frac{\log h}{2\pi i}\delta .
1313: \]
1314: Their overlap is the literal identity
1315: \[
1316:                     F+o_{\rm ord}-o_{\rm cap}=F_0.   \tag{SZ.50}
1317: \]
1318: After a positive \(q_\infty\) continuation,
1319: \(o_{\rm ord}\) changes by \(-nk\,t\delta\).
1320: The coordinate \(\eta=\zeta-o_{\rm ord}\) therefore changes
1321: by \(+nk\,t\delta\), which multiplies its line coordinate by
1322: \(v^{nk}\). This is exactly the shift of the full chain by
1323: \(nk\) in (SZ.47), together with the retained \(A_0\) map.
1324: Only after that continuation is included is the chain
1325: quotiented by its scalar period.
1326: 
1327: The parameter deck is equally explicit. It is
1328: \[
1329:  R_{\rm ord}=q_1^{nk},\qquad R_{\rm cap}=h^{nk},
1330: \]
1331: and the actual square is
1332: \[
1333:                         F_{t+1}R_{\rm ord}
1334:                               =R_{\rm cap}F_t.       \tag{SZ.51}
1335: \]
1336: Indeed \(F_{t+1}/F_t=q_\infty^{nk}\).
1337: The cap unit acts on each node chart by
1338: \[
1339:            (X_j,Y_j)\longmapsto(h^{nk}X_j,h^{-nk}Y_j)
1340: \]
1341: with unchanged index and unchanged finite root map. It extends
1342: at \(v=0\), where \(h=1\). The origin \(o_{\rm cap}\) has
1343: exactly the change \(nk\log(h)/(2\pi i)\delta\), so it
1344: makes this actual deck pure. The ordinary origin does the
1345: same for \(R_{\rm ord}\). Thus (SZ.50) descends in both
1346: directions, with all units and signed cap characters retained.
1347: Here subtraction of \(o_{\rm cap}\) defines the new periodic
1348: coordinates on the punctured logarithmic cover. It does not
1349: assert that \(\exp(t\log h)\) is holomorphic at \(v=0\) in
1350: the old coordinates. The change is multiplication by a scalar
1351: depending only on the invariant base \((q_\infty,v)\), so it
1352: commutes with every actual source translation, line map and
1353: finite root map in \(H_0\). Consequently the new chain atlas
1354: carries exactly that same \(H_0\), extends in (SZ.49), and
1355: has the literal extending overlap \(F_0\).
1356: It is the precise chain/origin comparison used in P2.8,
1357: applied here to the displayed varying source and line.
1358: 
1359: These charts give the finite-base partial attachment away
1360: from the collision. They do not complete the collision fan,
1361: identify its oriented boundary components, or assign a
1362: source-loop return. The exact period and signed origin
1363: identities are executed in
1364: code/part3_fourfold/source_zero_units_caps.py and its JSON/output.
1365: 
1366: ## 22. Analytic quotient of the period-equivariant collision atlas
1367: 
1368: This is the analytic assembly for the explicit sector fans in
1369: the root's source-zero prism note and the independent
1370: source-zero prism audit. The source-loop characteristic
1371: calculation is still separate. For the default selected cusp,
1372: the I-face uses the basis \(e_1,-e_2\), so its boundary
1373: triangles have the required diagonal \(e_1-e_2\). The earlier
1374: \(e_1,e_2\) choice gives the separately specified positive
1375: fan of Section 15. These two boundary markings are not
1376: identified by a source-loop assertion.
1377: 
1378: On the E face prescribe all rays
1379: \((0,j,1,0)\), \(j\in\mathbb Z\), before extending the
1380: common H refinement. The period \(g\) shifts \(j\) by \(N\).
1381: These are precisely the primitive height-one rays of the
1382: chain (SZ.49); the raw rays with \(j\) divisible by \(N\)
1383: would omit its actual component charts. The W face already
1384: has primitive consecutive rays in its image lattice, and
1385: the I face is the retained unimodular cusp fan. The common
1386: H overlay retains its primitive integral ray multiples.
1387: In particular its rational height-one drawing does not
1388: declare the resulting central divisor reduced.
1389: 
1390: The analytic toric charts are the affine semigroup charts
1391: of that actual common fan, pulled back by the displayed
1392: blowup coordinates on the base. A period indexed by
1393: \(n\in\mathbb Z^2\) has the lattice map
1394: \[
1395:  (x,r,s)\longmapsto
1396:           (x+rP_0n+sHn,r,s)
1397: \]
1398: on the \((P_0,H)\) sector. Its remaining factors are the
1399: units in (SZ.44). Multiplication by an extending base unit
1400: acts on every toric character and its inverse character
1401: by the same literal unit powers, so it is an analytic
1402: isomorphism of each corresponding semigroup chart.
1403: The unit factors are homomorphisms in \(n\), because they
1404: are products of the two commuting periods in (SZ.44).
1405: Hence the full period maps, including their inverses,
1406: give an honest action on this analytic atlas. The common
1407: H refinement is period-equivariant, so these actions
1408: agree on every overlap.
1409: 
1410: Here is a compact-fundamental-cover argument which also
1411: retains the units. For a nonzero base value, let
1412: \(L\) be the real two-by-two matrix of logarithmic
1413: magnitudes of the actual two periods. On a corner
1414: sector it has the form
1415: \[
1416:                         L=-rP_0-sH+C ,
1417: \]
1418: where \(r,s\) are the positive logarithmic radii of its
1419: two base coordinates and \(C\) is bounded. This follows
1420: from (SZ.44) and the extending base units. The fixed
1421: constant \(\lambda_0\) is part of that bound.
1422: On a sufficiently small corner polydisc \(r,s\) are
1423: large. In the I sector the determinant polynomial and
1424: the norm of \(rI+sH\) give a lower singular-value bound
1425: by a positive constant times \(r+s\). In either rank-one
1426: sector its determinant is a positive constant times
1427: \(s(r+s)\), while its norm is bounded by a constant
1428: times \(r+s\); its lower singular value is therefore
1429: bounded below by a positive constant times \(s\).
1430: Thus the bounded matrix \(C\) does not destroy
1431: invertibility there.
1432: 
1433: Use the actual parallelogram
1434: \(L([0,1]^2)\) for the logarithmic magnitudes, with
1435: compact angular coordinates. The corresponding
1436: parallelogram for \(-rP_0-sH\) differs from it by a
1437: uniformly bounded set. In the I sector the inverse
1438: piecewise-linear prism map has only finitely many
1439: simplex types modulo periods; the determinant bounds
1440: just given keep this bounded perturbation in finitely
1441: many adjacent cell types. In the rank-one sectors this
1442: same conclusion follows directly from the strip
1443: assignment (PF.8)--(PF.10): its strip width is
1444: \(s+rc\), its vertical height is \(s\), and the bounded
1445: perturbation changes the two assigned integers by a
1446: bounded amount. The parallelogram and strip differ
1447: by only a bounded number of vertical translates,
1448: independent of \(r/s\); this follows by comparing
1449: the linear function \(rd\,x/(s+rc)\) with the two
1450: linear pieces of the displayed \(f(x)\).
1451: 
1452: Consequently a fixed finite set of cone representatives
1453: and their adjacent charts covers the actual quotient.
1454: The logarithmic magnitudes in their monomial coordinate
1455: charts have a uniform upper bound. Their angular parts
1456: lie in compact tori. Closing these coordinate regions,
1457: including their toric faces, therefore gives compact
1458: sets over a closed smaller base polydisc. The same
1459: argument near an interior point of the exceptional
1460: base curve uses the full-rank matrix H alone.
1461: 
1462: At a rank-one boundary the remaining noncompact
1463: direction is exactly its kernel period, rather than
1464: an additional missing toric origin. In the W sector,
1465: use the actual line-norm shell for (SZ.45), with the
1466: uniform strict estimate (SZ.46). In the E sector,
1467: use the Tate shell for \(Z\mapsto QZ\).
1468: Adding these shells to the preceding finite toric
1469: chart cover gives a compact fundamental cover at
1470: the two rank-one faces as well.
1471: At their intersections with H the same kernel
1472: vectors have nonzero H image, because
1473: \(\det H=2N\ne0\). Hence an unbounded sequence of
1474: kernel translates cannot accumulate at a fixed
1475: H-cone chart: the full-rank cone separation
1476: controls the closures of the face shells.
1477: This verifies compatibility of the shell cover
1478: with the exceptional-face compact cover.
1479: 
1480: These same charts prove proper discontinuity and the
1481: Hausdorff property. On a full-rank face, a nonzero
1482: period cannot stabilize a bounded polyhedral cell,
1483: because it translates that cell by the nonzero
1484: vector \(P_0n\) or \(Hn\). Only finitely many
1485: translates of a compact chart meet it. On a rank-one
1486: face its possible stabilizer is the explicitly
1487: identified kernel; its strict norm contraction
1488: meets a compact shell only for finitely many
1489: iterates. The estimates are uniform on the
1490: neighbouring charts. Thus the full equivalence
1491: relation is locally a finite union of closed
1492: graphs on these compact covers. Its quotient is
1493: Hausdorff, and inverse images of compact smaller
1494: base sets are compact.
1495: 
1496: Over \(v\ne0\) the chart comparison is the specified
1497: family, not only a birational torus comparison.
1498: On the selected divisor the I-face is its chosen
1499: fan. On the other nodal divisor the W-face is the
1500: actual source Tate chain and degree-zero line
1501: with gluing \(c=(QP)^a\), followed by the actual
1502: kernel contraction (SZ.45). This is the ordinary
1503: nonzero-line source filling under the explicit
1504: theta/Cartier map of Section 19. The E face and
1505: every H modification lie over \(v=0\).
1506: The finite-base pieces have the two literal deck
1507: squares (SZ.50)--(SZ.51) in their changed ordinary
1508: coordinate \(\eta\). The collision fan above uses
1509: the original Tate coordinate \(V\). Their common
1510: physical delta character is retained, but the
1511: coordinate change between these two central
1512: atlases still has to be extended. Its exact deck
1513: is recorded below.
1514: 
1515: This proves the local normal analytic collision
1516: quotient. Section 21 proves the finite-base partial
1517: attachment in its own chain coordinates. Their
1518: global central attachment is a separate remaining
1519: map. A
1520: period-equivariant toric resolution can subsequently
1521: be performed relative to the already smooth I,
1522: W and primitive E boundary subfans. Its new ray
1523: multiplicities must be retained. The resulting
1524: oriented boundary, the fixed-stratum incidence
1525: and the characteristic pairing have not been
1526: computed in this subsection; none is replaced
1527: by the determinant or fundamental-region count.
1528: 
1529: The base multiplicities are also literal. Since
1530: \(q_1-q_2=T_1(v)-T_0(v)=v\,u_v(v)\), with
1531: \(u_v(0)=4\), the I corner has
1532: \(q_1=xy,q_2=y\) and
1533: \(v=y(x-1)/u_v(v)\); the W corner has
1534: \(q_2=xy,q_1=y\) and
1535: \(v=y(1-x)/u_v(v)\). The E corner uses
1536: \(v=xy,q_1=y\). Thus an integral ray with
1537: base coordinates \((r,s)\) has \(v\)-order
1538: \(s,s,r+s\) in the I, W, E sectors respectively.
1539: These orders apply to the primitive integral
1540: rays of the actual refinement. They retain
1541: every higher H multiplicity. Each normal
1542: toric chart is the closure of its dense
1543: torus over the nonzero parameter, so no
1544: total-space component is contained in the
1545: central fibre, and its
1546: parameter function is a nonzero divisor.
1547: Its local rings are therefore torsion free
1548: over the one-dimensional parameter ring,
1549: which proves flatness of this local quotient over
1550: the \(v\)-disc.
1551: 
1552: **Scope correction, recorded 2026-11-17 after the actual
1553: chain-deck comparison.** The previous version of this
1554: paragraph invoked \(F_0\) to assemble the collision
1555: fan and finite caps globally. It had not supplied
1556: the central transition from the Tate \(V\) atlas
1557: to \(\eta=\zeta-o_{\rm ord}\). That invocation is
1558: retained here as an identified incomplete step,
1559: not as a proved compact global filling. At fixed
1560: old \(\zeta\), positive \(q_1\) continuation gives
1561: \[
1562:  \eta\longmapsto\eta-nk\,t\,\delta,\qquad
1563:        V_\eta\longmapsto v^{-nk}V_\eta .                    \tag{SZ.51a}
1564:  \]
1565: Its inverse overlap has \(v^{nk}\). Positive
1566: \(q_\infty\) continuation instead gives \(v^{nk}\),
1567: as in Section 21. These arrows retain their signs.
1568: The literal \(h,g\) collision fan has not yet been
1569: identified with a central atlas carrying (SZ.51a).
1570: The local regularization and the compact interior/H
1571: normal calculations below do not use that missing
1572: global attachment. No compact global \(W\), its
1573: signature, or a source-loop return is assigned here.
1574: 
1575: ## 23. Remaining original-u cap-root endpoint dictionary
1576: 
1577: Sections 19--22 construct the literal marked \((v,T)\) family,
1578: with its cap root defined by \(z_{\rm new}^m=T^{-1}\).
1579: The comparison to a source loop in the original normalized
1580: \((u,t)\) presentation retains a further root arrow. The exact
